<h2>The prosecution's spine: one statement</h2>
<p>FIR No. 527/1998 of PS Kalyan Puri began as a case under Sections 498A, 307 and allied provisions and was converted on Dayawati's death. The trial court at Karkardooma convicted the husband, his mother and others under Sections 302/34, 201/34 and 498A/34 IPC in July 2002. By the time the appeals were heard, several family witnesses had died in an accident, others had turned hostile, and everything rested on the statement the SDM recorded on 22 October 1998 — in question-answer form, after a doctor declared the patient fit — in which Dayawati described harassment over demands for Rs 50,000 on two occasions and a Maruti car, and said kerosene was poured on her before she was set alight.</p>

<h2>The attack on the declaration, and why it failed</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>"100% burns means no statement":</strong> rejected on the medical evidence that shock-induced disorientation is corrected by resuscitation and that such burns do not impair the brain; percentage alone is not determinative (Dal Singh).</li>
  <li><strong>The thumb impression:</strong> nearly blank — explained by degloving, and taken only as a precaution; certification by a doctor is a rule of caution, not an inflexible statutory condition (Govindappa).</li>
  <li><strong>Timing discrepancies:</strong> witnesses deposed over two years after the event; the court applied Wassan Singh and quoted the aphorism that perfect truths are seldom found, but perfect lies often are.</li>
  <li><strong>The suicide theory:</strong> rested on a history given by the husband himself to the doctors and found no support; the trial court's conclusion that the defence story "does not just stack up" was affirmed.</li>
  <li><strong>The alibi:</strong> the Bhaiya-Duj travel claim was supported only by a relative and no independent evidence of the journey.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<h2>Where the prosecution failed: attribution</h2>
<p>The same rigour that sustained the murder counts dismantled the cruelty counts against the women of the household. The declaration's collective "they", and hearsay from a neighbour whose wife — the direct recipient of the deceased's confidences — was never examined, could not fix individual liability. The mother-in-law was acquitted under Section 498A though her Section 302/34 conviction stood; the sister-in-law's conviction under Section 498A/34 was set aside entirely, the court holding that conviction cannot be upheld on the basis of guesswork.</p>

<h2>The outcomes</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Tilak Raj (husband)</strong><br>Convictions under Sections 302, 201 and 498A upheld; directed to surrender within three working days.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Anokhi Devi (mother-in-law)</strong><br>Sections 302/201 upheld, 498A acquitted; at 77, sentence modified to the five years, seven months already served.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Raj Kumari (sister-in-law)</strong><br>Section 498A/34 conviction set aside.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Proclaimed offenders</strong><br>The Commissioner of Police directed to pursue the two absconders, with monthly progress reports to the trial court.</div>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> The judgment is a working manual on dying declarations: fitness certification, the recorder's independence, the form of recording, and the physical condition of the document all featured — and the Bench's own inspection of the record shows how appellate courts test these instruments decades later. Equally instructive is its discipline on attribution: collective pronouns in a dying declaration convict no one in particular, and the acquittals here turned on exactly that line.</p>
</div>
